{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 20}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 104, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "A residual connection boosts conv-heavy cells but hurts pooling-dominated cells, as in experiments 3 vs 4."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "When another identity path to the output already exists, an extra skip adds little and dilutes learned features."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Adding a skip on edge 4 mainly helps by giving gradient a shortcut, which is redundant when edge 3 is already a residual (experiments 9 vs 10)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Without the input-output residual, removing edge 5 costs more capacity and gradient flow (experiments 1 vs 2)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 4 as skip, pooled node-1 features reach the output with no conv to process them (experiments 13 vs 14)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing the residual skip with a conv loses the residual's optimization benefit, unlike replacing none or pooling."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a conv where there was no edge adds capacity and a path, whereas swapping out a 3x3 conv is roughly neutral."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "A skip into node 2 is redundant and replaces a useful conv when an input-to-output skip already exists (experiments 19 vs 20)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "co